1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,552
JESS:

2
00:00:01,552 --> 00:00:03,990
How are you getting on with your art project, Tom?

3
00:00:03,990 --> 00:00:04,633
TOM:

4
00:00:04,633 --> 00:00:04,834
OK.

5
00:00:04,834 --> 00:00:08,652
Like, they gave us the theme of birds to base our project on,

6
00:00:08,652 --> 00:00:12,310
and I'm not really all that interested in wildlife.

7
00:00:12,310 --> 00:00:14,550
But I'm starting to get into it.

8
00:00:14,550 --> 00:00:17,420
I've pretty well finished the introductory stage.

9
00:00:17,420 --> 00:00:18,042
JESS:

10
00:00:18,042 --> 00:00:18,524
So have I.

11
00:00:18,524 --> 00:00:22,177
When they gave us all those handouts with details of books

12
00:00:22,177 --> 00:00:25,349
and websites to look at, I was really put off,

13
00:00:25,349 --> 00:00:28,520
but the more I read, the more interested I got.

14
00:00:28,520 --> 00:00:28,570
TOM:

15
00:00:28,570 --> 00:00:29,766
Me too.

16
00:00:29,766 --> 00:00:35,164
I found I could research so many different aspects of birds in art –

17
00:00:35,164 --> 00:00:36,410
colour, movement, texture.

18
00:00:36,410 --> 00:00:39,990
So I was looking forward to the Bird Park visit.

19
00:00:39,990 --> 00:00:40,365
JESS:

20
00:00:40,365 --> 00:00:41,489
What a letdown!

21
00:00:41,489 --> 00:00:45,610
It poured with rain and we hardly saw a single bird.

22
00:00:45,610 --> 00:00:49,530
Much less use than the trip to the Natural History Museum.

23
00:00:49,530 --> 00:00:49,887
TOM:

24
00:00:49,887 --> 00:00:50,244
Yeah.

25
00:00:50,244 --> 00:00:53,100
I liked all the stuff about evolution there.

26
00:00:53,100 --> 00:00:58,910
The workshop sessions with Dr Fletcher were good too, especially the brainstorming sessions.

27
00:00:58,910 --> 00:00:59,269
JESS:

28
00:00:59,269 --> 00:01:01,782
I missed those because I was ill.

29
00:01:01,782 --> 00:01:05,730
I wish we could've seen the projects last year's students did.

30
00:01:05,730 --> 00:01:06,029
TOM:

31
00:01:06,029 --> 00:01:06,329
Mm.

32
00:01:06,329 --> 00:01:09,920
I suppose they want us to do our own thing, not copy.

33
00:01:09,920 --> 00:01:10,277
JESS:

34
00:01:10,277 --> 00:01:12,420
Have you drafted your proposal yet?

35
00:01:12,420 --> 00:01:12,738
TOM:

36
00:01:12,738 --> 00:01:13,055
Yes,

37
00:01:13,055 --> 00:01:14,960
but I haven't handed it in.

38
00:01:14,960 --> 00:01:17,630
I need to amend some parts.

39
00:01:17,630 --> 00:01:23,782
I've realised the notes from my research are almost all just descriptions, I haven't

40
00:01:23,782 --> 00:01:25,100
actually evaluated anything.

41
00:01:25,100 --> 00:01:26,850
So I'll have to fix that.

42
00:01:26,850 --> 00:01:27,134
JESS:

43
00:01:27,134 --> 00:01:29,690
Oh, I didn't know we had to do that.

44
00:01:29,690 --> 00:01:31,650
I'll have to look at that too.

45
00:01:31,650 --> 00:01:34,830
Did you do a timeline for the project?

46
00:01:34,830 --> 00:01:35,158
TOM:

47
00:01:35,158 --> 00:01:35,487
Yes,

48
00:01:35,487 --> 00:01:36,800
and a mind map.

49
00:01:36,800 --> 00:01:37,180
JESS:

50
00:01:37,180 --> 00:01:37,560
Yeah,

51
00:01:37,560 --> 00:01:38,700
so did I.

52
00:01:38,700 --> 00:01:40,220
I quite enjoyed that.

53
00:01:40,220 --> 00:01:44,402
But it was hard having to explain the basis for my decisions in my

54
00:01:44,402 --> 00:01:45,000
action plan.

55
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:45,455
TOM:

56
00:01:45,455 --> 00:01:45,910
What?

57
00:01:45,910 --> 00:01:46,220
JESS:

58
00:01:46,220 --> 00:01:47,770
You know, give a rationale.

59
00:01:47,770 --> 00:01:48,020
TOM:

60
00:01:48,020 --> 00:01:50,020
I didn't realise we had to do that.

61
00:01:50,020 --> 00:01:53,000
OK, I can add it now.

62
00:01:53,000 --> 00:01:55,628
And I've done the video diary presentation,

63
00:01:55,628 --> 00:02:00,510
and worked out what I want my outcome to be in the project.

64
00:02:00,510 --> 00:02:00,816
JESS:

65
00:02:00,816 --> 00:02:05,103
Someone told me it's best not to be too precise about your actual outcome

66
00:02:05,103 --> 00:02:06,022
at this stage,

67
00:02:06,022 --> 00:02:09,390
so you have more scope to explore your ideas later on.

68
00:02:09,390 --> 00:02:13,459
So I'm going to go back to my proposal to make it a bit

69
00:02:13,459 --> 00:02:14,040
more vague.

70
00:02:14,040 --> 00:02:14,469
TOM:

71
00:02:14,469 --> 00:02:14,897
Really?

72
00:02:14,897 --> 00:02:17,470
OK, I'll change that too then.

73
00:02:17,470 --> 00:02:17,868
TOM:

74
00:02:17,868 --> 00:02:24,228
One part of the project I'm unsure about is where we choose some paintings of birds

75
00:02:24,228 --> 00:02:27,010
and say what they mean to us.

76
00:02:27,010 --> 00:02:30,720
Like, I chose a painting of a falcon by Landseer.

77
00:02:30,720 --> 00:02:35,363
I like it because the bird's standing there with his head turned to one

78
00:02:35,363 --> 00:02:35,695
side,

79
00:02:35,695 --> 00:02:38,680
but he seems to be staring straight at you.

80
00:02:38,680 --> 00:02:42,760
But I can't just say it's a bit scary, can I?

81
00:02:42,760 --> 00:02:43,234
JESS:

82
00:02:43,234 --> 00:02:48,920
You could talk about the possible danger suggested by the bird's look.

83
00:02:48,920 --> 00:02:49,387
TOM:

84
00:02:49,387 --> 00:02:50,320
Oh, OK.

85
00:02:50,320 --> 00:02:50,699
JESS:

86
00:02:50,699 --> 00:02:54,870
There's a picture of a fish hawk by Audubon I like.

87
00:02:54,870 --> 00:02:58,935
It's swooping over the water with a fish in its talons,

88
00:02:58,935 --> 00:03:03,370
and with great black wings which take up most of the picture.

89
00:03:03,370 --> 00:03:03,705
TOM:

90
00:03:03,705 --> 00:03:07,730
So you could discuss it in relation to predators and food chains?

91
00:03:07,730 --> 00:03:08,091
JESS:

92
00:03:08,091 --> 00:03:13,140
Well actually I think I'll concentrate on the impression of rapid motion it gives.

93
00:03:13,140 --> 00:03:13,650
TOM:

94
00:03:13,650 --> 00:03:14,160
Right.

95
00:03:14,160 --> 00:03:14,480
JESS:

96
00:03:14,480 --> 00:03:18,647
Do you know that picture of a kingfisher by van Gogh – it's perching

97
00:03:18,647 --> 00:03:20,890
on a reed growing near a stream.

98
00:03:20,890 --> 00:03:21,267
TOM:

99
00:03:21,267 --> 00:03:25,420
Yes it's got these beautiful blue and red and black shades.

100
00:03:25,420 --> 00:03:25,770
JESS:

101
00:03:25,770 --> 00:03:26,471
Mm hm.

102
00:03:26,471 --> 00:03:31,376
I've actually chosen it because I saw a real kingfisher once when I was

103
00:03:31,376 --> 00:03:34,178
little, I was out walking with my grandfather,

104
00:03:34,178 --> 00:03:35,930
and I've never forgotten it.

105
00:03:35,930 --> 00:03:36,284
TOM:

106
00:03:36,284 --> 00:03:38,760
So we can use a personal link?

107
00:03:38,760 --> 00:03:39,040
JESS:

108
00:03:39,040 --> 00:03:39,320
Sure.

109
00:03:39,320 --> 00:03:39,732
TOM:

110
00:03:39,732 --> 00:03:40,143
OK.

111
00:03:40,143 --> 00:03:44,673
There's a portrait called William Wells, I can't remember the artist

112
00:03:44,673 --> 00:03:48,790
but it's a middle-aged man who's just shot a bird.

113
00:03:48,790 --> 00:03:49,834
And his expression,

114
00:03:49,834 --> 00:03:54,708
and the way he's holding the bird in his hand suggests he's not sure

115
00:03:54,708 --> 00:03:56,100
about what he's done.

116
00:03:56,100 --> 00:04:01,980
To me it's about how ambiguous people are in the way they exploit the

117
00:04:01,980 --> 00:04:02,820
natural world.

118
00:04:02,820 --> 00:04:03,508
JESS:

119
00:04:03,508 --> 00:04:04,197
Interesting.

120
00:04:04,197 --> 00:04:06,950
There's Gauguin's picture Vairumati.

121
00:04:06,950 --> 00:04:09,240
He did it in Tahiti.

122
00:04:09,240 --> 00:04:14,576
It's a woman with a white bird behind her that is eating a lizard,

123
00:04:14,576 --> 00:04:19,150
and what I'm interested in is what idea this bird refers to.

124
00:04:19,150 --> 00:04:23,720
Apparently, it's a reference to the never-ending cycle of existence.

125
00:04:23,720 --> 00:04:24,135
TOM:

126
00:04:24,135 --> 00:04:24,549
Wow.

127
00:04:24,549 --> 00:04:29,110
I chose a portrait of a little boy, Giovanni de Medici.

128
00:04:29,110 --> 00:04:32,380
He's holding a tiny bird in one fist.

129
00:04:32,380 --> 00:04:36,680
I like the way he's holding it carefully so he doesn't hurt it.

130
00:04:36,680 --> 00:04:37,453
JESS:

131
00:04:37,453 --> 00:04:39,000
Ah right.
